[Music]  we're so used to seeing studio logos at  the beginning of movies like paramount  mountain Disney's castle and DreamWorks  Moon but do you know the true stories  behind each of them watch the video to  find out who's the little boy in  DreamWorks logo if this is your first  time visiting our channel don't forget  to subscribe and please give this video  a big thumbs up today we're sharing 10  true stories behind Hollywood studio  logos Walt Disney Company the Magic  Castle logo is synonymous with Walt  Disney movies but believe it or not the  company didn't bring in that logo until  1985 Walt Disney was established 62  years before that in 1923 before 1985 it  just used different stylized versions of  the words Walt Disney presents the  castle logo has gone through some  changes over the years too in 1990 it  was a simple White Castle revealing  itself on a blue background and in 2006  it became a very detailed scene based on  a real building in Europe  metro-goldwyn-mayer the  metro-goldwyn-mayer roaring lion is kind  of terrifying when you're a kid  publicist Howard Diaz originally  designed it for Goldwyn picture  corporation he based it on the athletics  club mascot of his alma mater Columbia  University the logo stayed when three  Studios merged to become MGM in 1924  several lines have been used over the  years including slats jackie kelly  coffee  Tanner George and now Leo the motto on  the logo ARS gratia artis is Latin for  art for art's sake Columbia the torch  lady in Columbia's logo has undergone  various changes since 1924 she started  out dressed as a Roman soldier  she was then draped in an American flag  which was later replaced by a blue drape  in the 1990s illustrator Michael Diaz  redesigned the logo do you know what  inspired Diaz when he was painting the  model Jenny Joseph was simply holding up  a regular lamp during her lunch break it  is surprising to see the result when we  think about this Warner Brothers the  Warner Bros logo has gone through a few  changes over the years the first version  in 1923 head WB on the bottom half of a  shield with a photo of the Burbank  studio building on the top half the WB  then filled the shield in front of a  cloud filled sky from the 19  Bertie's as it does today but from 1972  and 1984 at the studio dropped the  shield and used a W in a circle this  became the logo for Warner Music Group  Pixar the lab in the Pixar logo was  actually the star of the corporation's  first short movie in that two minutes  shorts a lamp called luxo jr. wants to  play ball with his father lexo senior he  accidentally deflates the ball the short  ends with luxo jr. bringing in a much  bigger beach ball in the logo luxo jr.  is cordless and he jumps down on top of  the I of the word Pixar like you stomped  on the ball by the way that ball shows  up in most Pixar films did you see this  fall in one of your favorite Pixar  movies tell us where you saw it in the  comments 20th Century Fox Fox Film  corporation and 20th century pictures  merged in 1935 to create 20th Century  Fox artist Emile cosas jr. designed the  logo as an art deco monolith with  searchlights when Fox began releasing  movies in widescreen in the 1950s the 0  in 20th was tilted so the monolith still  looks proportional the zero got  straightened again in the 1980s the logo  was recreated in CGI in 1994 and again  in 2008 United Artists the United  Artists logo has gone through lots of  different designs it initially stayed  the same for a long time from 1919 to  the late 1960s it was simply the words  United Artists inside a hexagon then it  went very colorful for a couple of years  blue lines forming at E were introduced  to the logo in the early 1970s it went  gold then back to the blue t and then  had a redesign in the early 1980s a blue  line revealed the initials UA then it  went back to selling out the whole two  words in the 1990s then it reverted to  UA this time in silver after so many  changes it is difficult to recognize  United Artists over the years paramount  power mounts majestic mountain logo has  been around since the corporation's  beginning in fact it's Hollywood's  oldest surviving movie studio logo and  it all started with the doodle  WW Hodgkinson sketched the ben lomond  mountain from his beautiful childhood  and that became the logo later versions  of the mountain are thought to be based  on a peak in the Peruvian Andes there  were originally  twenty-four stars around the peak one  for each of the 24 actors under contract  in 1916 but in the more recent versions  that number reduced to 22 Universal the  globe has remained a constant feature of  the universal logo but it's gone through  various transformation from the 1920s a  plane circled around a spinning globe  it left a smoke trail that spells out  the studio's names  sparkling stars were added in the 1930s  as the years went by computer-generated  versions of the clove appeared like in  its 100th anniversary logo the globe has  been set against sunrise and sunset  DreamWorks when Steven Spielberg founded  DreamWorks in 1994 he had a vision of a  computer-generated man fishing from the  moon for the logo visual effects  supervisor Dennis Muren suggested it  should be hand-painted artist Robert  hunt did the honors he also did a  version with a boy instead of a man  using his son William as the model and  that's the version they went with how  about the SKG  it stands for Spielberg and founders  captain Berg and Gavin's  which of these true stories behind  Hollywood studio logos did you find the  most interesting let us know in the  comments and remember to give us a big  thumbs up  [Music]

